<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nalanda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nalanda was a great Buddhist monastery and centre of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which flourished from the 5th to the 12th century. Supported by the Gupta kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and the Pala Empire, it drew students from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, including the Chinese pilgrims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yijing">Yijing</a>. It declined after attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s forces around 1200, and its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nalanda.jpg" alt="Nalanda" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Odantapuribs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_No.-_3,_Nalanda_Archaeological_Site.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
